---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Quick Start | Honeybee-PH | PH-Tools" activePage="honeybee-ph">
  <div class="content-page">
    <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/honeybee-ph/">Honeybee-PH</a> / Quick Start</p>
    <h1>Quick start</h1>
    <p>
      This walkthrough shows the shortest path from a basic
      <a href="https://www.rhino3d.com/" target="_blank">Rhino / Grasshopper</a>
      Honeybee model to WUFI-Passive and PHPP.
    </p>

    <img src="/img/quick-start/quick_start_diagram.svg" alt="Basic usage diagram: Grasshopper to WUFI-Passive or PHPP" />

    <p>
      For full project tutorials, see <a href="/honeybee-ph/resources/">Resources</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Create the HB-Model</h2>
    <p>
      Start with a valid Honeybee model. This example uses a simple box built with standard
      Grasshopper geometry and regular Honeybee components. No Honeybee-PH components yet.
    </p>
    <img src="/img/quick-start/basic_honeybee_model.png" alt="Basic Honeybee model in Grasshopper" />

    <p>Previewing the model shows a box with four walls, a roof, and a floor:</p>
    <img src="/img/quick-start/basic_honeybee_vis.png" alt="Basic Honeybee model preview" />

    <h2>2. HB-Model &rarr; HBJSON</h2>
    <p>
      Write the Honeybee model to an HBJSON file with the standard "HB Dump Objects" component.
      Still plain Honeybee, no Honeybee-PH components yet.
    </p>
    <img src="/img/quick-start/basic_honeybee_to_HBJSON.png" alt="Write Honeybee model to HBJSON" />

    <h2>3a. HBJSON &rarr; WUFI-Passive</h2>
    <p>
      Now bring in Honeybee-PH. Drop the "HBPH - Write WUFI XML" component onto the canvas.
      It reads the HBJSON file and writes out a WUFI-XML file.
    </p>
    <img src="/img/quick-start/basic_honeybee_to_WUFI.png" alt="Export to WUFI-XML" />

    <p>Open that WUFI-XML file in WUFI-Passive via "File / Open..."</p>
    <img src="/img/quick-start/WUFI_open.png" alt="Open WUFI-Passive" />

    <p>
      Browse to where you saved the file and select it. If you don't see it, switch the file filter to
      <code>*.xml</code>.
    </p>
    <img src="/img/quick-start/WUFI_XML.png" alt="Navigate to WUFI-XML file" />

    <p>Click "Open" and the geometry will appear in the WUFI-Passive preview panel.</p>
    <img src="/img/quick-start/WUFI_success.png" alt="WUFI-Passive preview showing the model" />

    <h2>3b. HBJSON &rarr; PHPP</h2>
    <p>
      Exporting to PHPP works the same way, with one extra requirement. Because PHPP is an Excel workbook,
      the target PHPP file must be <strong>open at the same time</strong> as Grasshopper.
    </p>

    <p>Open a blank PHPP workbook alongside Rhino and Grasshopper:</p>
    <img src="/img/quick-start/PHPP_open.png" alt="Open PHPP alongside Grasshopper" />

    <p>
      With both open, run the "HBPH - Write to PHPP" component. It writes directly to the active Excel
      workbook, so close any other Excel files first if you run into trouble.
    </p>
    <img src="/img/quick-start/PHPP_success.png" alt="PHPP with model data" />

    <h2>Next steps</h2>
    <p>
      This box is the bare minimum, with basic geometry and default attributes. From here, you can finish
      the model in WUFI-Passive or PHPP, or use the Honeybee-PH components to define windows, mechanical
      systems, and thermal bridges directly in Grasshopper.
    </p>
    <p>
      For full component walkthroughs, see <a href="/honeybee-ph/resources/">Resources</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
